HOW MUCH UPSTREAM CODE REMAINS
------------------------------

Measured at version 2.0.0, comparing custom_components/kasa_cloud against
upstream commit a568984:

    300 of 1858 lines (16%) are byte-identical to upstream.

Broken down, those 300 lines are:

    124  41%  blank lines
     89  30%  short fragments (see below)
     40  13%  import statements dictated by the Home Assistant API
     15   5%  decorators (@property)
     12   4%  method signatures required by Home Assistant's platform API
     11   4%  punctuation and block delimiters
      5   2%  docstring markers
      4   1%  module-level constants

The "short fragments" are Home Assistant scaffolding that any integration must
contain, in the form the framework requires:

    hass: HomeAssistant,
    async_add_entities: AddEntitiesCallback,
    ) -> None:
    def __init__(self, coordinator, device) -> None:
    async_add_entities(entities)
    await self.coordinator.async_request_refresh()
    return True

The only remaining candidates for original expression are the constant
BASE_URL = "https://wap.tplinkcloud.com", which is a factual endpoint address
rather than creative expression, and the class name KasaCloudDevice.

Every module was rewritten; coordinator.py, entity.py and the test suite are
new. CHANGES.md maps each change to the upstream line it replaced.

This measurement is reproducible:

    git remote add upstream https://github.com/onoffautomations/hassio_kasa_cloud
    git fetch upstream
    git diff --stat a568984 HEAD -- custom_components/

It is recorded here as a statement of fact about the code, not as a legal
conclusion. No part of this file is legal advice.


